@labor_day
# prose-audit: Grade A — One recurring file preserves each year's factual Labour Day form, reads the canonical union relationship at entry and makes party revival compete with union and anarchist ownership rather than erasing it.
title: May Day — who owns the workers' holiday?
subtitle: A recurring test of Lewica's relationship with organised labour
face-image: img/poland/events/union-march.webp
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 22
order: 30
max-visits: 7
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and year >= 2020 and year <= 2026 and month = 5 and last_labor_day_year < year
on-arrival: {!
Q.last_labor_day_year = Number(Q.year) || 2020;
Q.labor_day_entry_relationship = String(
    Q.labor_relationship_state || "ignored"
);
Q.labor_day_strategy = String(Q.labor_day_strategy || "No strategy");
Q.labor_day_union_years = Number(Q.labor_day_union_years) || 0;
Q.labor_day_revival_years = Number(Q.labor_day_revival_years) || 0;
Q.labor_day_autonomous_years = Number(Q.labor_day_autonomous_years) || 0;
Q.labor_day_ceremonial_years = Number(Q.labor_day_ceremonial_years) || 0;
Q.labor_day_party_conflicts = Number(Q.labor_day_party_conflicts) || 0;
Q.news_headline = "May Day tests who speaks for organised labour";
!}

= May Day [+ year +] — a small stage with several owners

[? if year = 2020: In 2019, **OPZZ** marked 1 May at the Warsaw Citadel while
radical-left and anarchist organisers held their own procession. The new
parliamentary Left did not inherit one mass march; it inherited several small
traditions suspicious of one another. This year pandemic restrictions reduce
the official observance to wreaths, statements and online meetings.?]
[? if year = 2021: A second pandemic May Day again produces wreaths rather
than a national procession. Lewica and OPZZ appear together at socialist
memorials, while workplace and anarcho-syndicalist organisers keep their own
networks alive away from the party cameras.?]
[? if year = 2022: After two years without a march, OPZZ, Nowa Lewica and
Razem return under **“Peace, Work, Security”**. They walk from the union
headquarters to Ignacy Daszyński's monument. A separate radical-left march
heads for the prime minister's office: revival has happened, but ownership
remains divided.?]
[? if year = 2023: OPZZ and Lewica gather several thousand people beneath the
Palace of Culture, walk one short street to the confederation's headquarters
and finish in an OPZZ family town. **Piotr Ostrowski**, the confederation's
chair, speaks about decent work; party leaders also turn the stage toward the
coming election.?]
[? if year = 2024: OPZZ, Nowa Lewica and Lewica Razem march for a **Social
Europe** and a shorter working week. Lewica now holds ministries, so every
promise meets a union question: which law, budget and inspector will make it
real? Other worker, communist and anarchist processions keep separate routes.?]
[? if year = 2025: The main OPZZ celebration moves to industrial **Łódź**.
Nowa Lewica joins its march and presidential stage; Razem campaigns separately
in Gdańsk. In Warsaw, tenant groups, workplace committees and anarchists march
for housing and a dismissed union activist without waiting for either party.?]
[? if year = 2026: OPZZ returns in Warsaw with flowers at the Citadel and an
educational gathering on the Vistula rather than a mass national march. Razem
speaks separately at the Gdańsk shipyard gate, while anarcho-syndicalists and
tenant organisers march through Praga against insecure work and rent extraction.?]

[? if labor_day_entry_relationship = "cooperative": **Our current movement
relationship is durable cooperation.** OPZZ and workplace organisers will
negotiate a joint route, but cooperation is not ownership: autonomous unions
and anarchists still reject a party procession wearing their history.?]
[? if labor_day_entry_relationship = "ignored": **Our current movement
relationship is consultation without durable cooperation.** Union leaders
expect another photograph followed by a year of unanswered demands. A party-
led revival will be treated as an attempted appropriation until the route and
platform belong to workers too.?]
[? if labor_day_entry_relationship = "hostile": **Our current movement
relationship is open conflict.** OPZZ and workplace organisers will not hand
Lewica their banners, and independent unions and anarchists already control
routes in several cities. Entering as their leader means entering against
them.?]

[? if labor_day_strategy != "No strategy": Last year we chose **[+
labor_day_strategy +]**. The people who supplied stewards, speakers and a route
remember whether that meant partnership or capture.?]

- @labor_day_institution
- @labor_day_union
- @labor_day_revive
- @labor_day_autonomous
- @labor_day_ceremony

@labor_day_institution
title: Let a permanent union congress lead a national May Day
subtitle: Four union-led years can revive the holiday without making it a party possession.
view-if: year = 2026 and labor_day_union_years >= 4
on-arrival: labor_day_strategy = "Permanent union congress"; labor_day_union_years += 1; labor_mobilization += 10; labor_credibility += 7; union_trust += 8; opzz_cooperation += 7; labor_dissent -= 7; workers_support += 5; organiser_energy += 4; public_trust += 3; labor_day_revival_years += 1; public_mood_pending_social_spending_support += 8; public_mood_pending_social_spending_salience += 7; news_headline = "A union congress turns May Day back into a national workers' march"

The confederations, workplace committees, tenant groups and autonomous unions
agree on demands before any party books a platform. Lewica pays for trains,
lawyers and accessibility, then marches behind the workplace delegations.
Anarchist organisers do not endorse parliament; they recognise enough control
over the route to join its anti-eviction and anti-repression bloc.

The result is the first genuinely national May Day of the period. The party is
visible because it helped organised labour become visible, not because it
replaced it.

- @return_to_dated_desk

@labor_day_union
title: Ask unions to write the route and demands; march behind them
subtitle: Build the canonical union relationship by surrendering the party's claim to the main stage.
on-arrival: {!
Q.labor_day_strategy = "Union-led march";
Q.labor_day_union_years += 1;
Q.labor_credibility += 5;
Q.labor_mobilization += 6;
Q.workers_support += 3;
Q.union_trust += Q.labor_day_entry_relationship == "hostile" ? 9 : 6;
Q.opzz_cooperation += Q.labor_day_entry_relationship == "hostile" ? 8 : 6;
Q.labor_dissent -= Q.labor_day_entry_relationship == "cooperative" ? 3 : 6;
Q.media_capacity += 1;
Q.public_mood_pending_social_spending_support += 6;
Q.public_mood_pending_social_spending_salience += 5;
Q.news_headline = "Lewica follows a May Day platform written by unions";
!}

OPZZ branches and workplace committees choose the disputes, speakers and
order of march. Lewica supplies parliamentary follow-up and local logistics;
its leaders do not turn the front row into a press conference.

[? if labor_day_entry_relationship = "cooperative": Existing cooperation
makes the division of labour look ordinary. The test comes after the march,
when union demands enter our programme and budget work.?]
[? if labor_day_entry_relationship = "ignored": Organisers accept the help
but keep the microphone. This is a first repair to a relationship built from
consultation without delivery.?]
[? if labor_day_entry_relationship = "hostile": OPZZ accepts only a written
non-interference agreement. The march does not end the conflict, but it creates
the first common procedure either side has trusted in months.?]

- @return_to_dated_desk

@labor_day_revive
title: Revive a national May Day under Lewica leadership
subtitle: Gain a party stage and meet resistance from unions and anarchists who already occupy the holiday.
on-arrival: {!
Q.labor_day_strategy = "Party-led revival";
Q.labor_day_revival_years += 1;
Q.labor_day_party_conflicts += 1;
Q.labor_mobilization += 8;
Q.media_capacity += 5;
Q.left_poll_momentum += 0.1;
Q.party_unity += 2;
Q.union_trust -= Q.labor_day_entry_relationship == "cooperative" ? 3 : 7;
Q.opzz_cooperation -= Q.labor_day_entry_relationship == "cooperative" ? 2 : 6;
Q.labor_dissent += Q.labor_day_entry_relationship == "hostile" ? 10 : 6;
Q.movement_autonomy -= 4;
Q.public_mood_pending_social_spending_support += 5;
Q.public_mood_pending_social_spending_salience += 6;
Q.news_headline = "Lewica's May Day revival opens a fight over who owns the holiday";
!}

Party offices book coaches, a national route and the largest sound system the
holiday has seen in years. The crowd grows, and so does the argument.

[? if labor_day_entry_relationship = "cooperative": OPZZ joins after winning
its own speaking order, but warns that cooperation was not permission to turn
the workers' holiday into a Lewica rally.?]
[? if labor_day_entry_relationship = "ignored": OPZZ sends branch banners but
refuses to endorse the platform. Workplace organisers call the revival an
election event assembled from demands the party previously left unanswered.?]
[? if labor_day_entry_relationship = "hostile": OPZZ keeps its distance and
several workplace committees organise a rival route. Our national procession
is larger than the recent ceremonies and weaker than the labour movement it
claims to lead.?]

In every case, anarchist and syndicalist groups denounce party capture and
defend their own processions in Warsaw, Poznań, Wrocław and the Tricity. We
have revived a spectacle; whether it becomes a workers' institution remains
someone else's verdict.

- @return_to_dated_desk

@labor_day_autonomous
title: Fund legal aid, stewards and transport for autonomous marches
subtitle: Strengthen workplace, tenant and anarchist organisers without asking them to carry party flags.
on-arrival: labor_day_strategy = "Autonomous movement support"; labor_day_autonomous_years += 1; resources -= 1; union_trust += 5; opzz_cooperation += 1; labor_mobilization += 7; labor_credibility += 4; labor_dissent -= 3; movement_autonomy += 7; organiser_energy += 5; progressive_credibility += 2; news_headline = "Independent May Day routes receive infrastructure without party control"
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The party lacks one resource for the legal and transport network.

Lewica lawyers defend assembly routes and dismissed organisers; local offices
pay for accessible transport and medics. The political organisations that
already keep May Day alive choose their own banners and attacks—including
attacks on us.

OPZZ gains a useful legal channel without a formal pact. Independent unions,
tenant groups and anarchists keep the space they built. Lewica receives less
television and a more credible answer when organisers ask what the party did
besides arrive for the photograph.

- @return_to_dated_desk

@labor_day_ceremony
title: Keep the wreaths, speeches and family picnic
subtitle: Avoid a fight over ownership; accept another year in which May Day remains politically minor.
on-arrival: labor_day_strategy = "Ceremonial observance"; labor_day_ceremonial_years += 1; barons_dissent -= 2; public_trust += 1; union_trust -= 2; opzz_cooperation -= 2; labor_mobilization -= 3; labor_credibility -= 3; labor_dissent += 3; organiser_energy += 1; public_mood_pending_social_spending_salience -= 3; news_headline = "May Day remains a ceremony rather than a national mobilisation"

Party leaders lay flowers, repeat the programme and join the union picnic.
Nobody can accuse Lewica of seizing a march because headquarters has not built
one. The modest observance is dignified and almost absent from the national
news.

Workplace and anarchist organisers retain their routes. Union officials record
that the parliamentary Left once again treated the movement's holiday as a
date in the commemorative calendar rather than an organising deadline.

- @return_to_dated_desk

@pride
# prose-audit: Grade A — The recurring June file distinguishes every Warsaw Pride from the 2019 record through 2026, branches its voice on live LGBT support and backlash, and makes corporate reach, rainbow-washing and worker power an explicit strategic conflict.
title: Pride — movement, party or brand?
subtitle: Warsaw's June parade tests the social climate and who owns equality
face-image: img/poland/events/pride-march-2025.webp
new-page: true
tags: poland_event
priority: 22
order: 30
max-visits: 7
view-if: continuous_campaign = 1 and year >= 2020 and year <= 2026 and month = 6 and last_pride_year < year
on-arrival: {!
const prideSupport = Number(Q.lgbt_equality_support) || 0;
const prideBacklash = Number(Q.lgbt_equality_backlash) || 0;
Q.pride_social_mood = prideSupport >= 60 && prideBacklash < 58
    ? "favourable"
    : (prideSupport >= 50 || prideSupport + 4 >= prideBacklash
        ? "contested" : "hostile");
Q.last_pride_year = Number(Q.year) || 2020;
Q.pride_strategy = String(Q.pride_strategy || "No strategy");
Q.pride_movement_years = Number(Q.pride_movement_years) || 0;
Q.pride_party_years = Number(Q.pride_party_years) || 0;
Q.pride_corporate_years = Number(Q.pride_corporate_years) || 0;
Q.pride_worker_compact_years = Number(Q.pride_worker_compact_years) || 0;
Q.news_headline = "Pride asks whether equality belongs to a movement, a party or a brand";
!}

= June [+ year +] — Pride in the climate we made

[? if year = 2020: The inherited benchmark is June 2019: Warsaw's first Pride
under mayoral patronage drew **47,000 by city estimates and nearly 80,000 by
the organisers' count**. Robert Biedroń, Włodzimierz Czarzasty and Razem all
marched among platforms from Netflix, Google, IBM, Microsoft, BNP Paribas,
Nielsen and MTV. This year's street parade is cancelled by COVID-19. The
argument moves online: mutual aid and visibility, or a branded Pride month
without a crowd.?]
[? if year = 2021: Pride returns on 19 June with masks and distancing. Rafał
Trzaskowski again grants patronage; Krzysztof Śmiszek and Wiosna join the
march. Many global brands that filled 2019's platforms stay physically away,
offering internal campaigns, discounts and donations instead.?]
[? if year = 2022: Warsaw shares its parade with **KyivPride**, displaced by
Russia's invasion. Ukrainian activists lead a march for equality and peace;
Robert Biedroń, Krzysztof Śmiszek and the Warsaw Left join them. Solidarity
with refugees now sits beside a Polish rights agenda still awaiting civil
recognition.?]
[? if year = 2023: The parade is dedicated to trans rights: a dignified legal
gender-recognition process, protection from hate and real access to care.
Lewica brings its own platform and senior leaders; diplomats and global firms
again fill the route. Visibility is no longer scarce, but legislation is.?]
[? if year = 2024: The Warsaw movement splits after the old organising team
calls a change of control at the Equality Volunteer Foundation a **hostile
takeover**. Three parades take place on 8, 15 and 22 June. Government Lewica
figures join the mayor-backed march; Razem pays to join the volunteer-led 22
June route. Activists must choose among community legitimacy, city patronage
and corporate logistics.?]
[? if year = 2025: The reunited flagship parade marches under **“The answer is
love”** with tens of thousands present. Equality Minister Katarzyna Kotula and
other Lewica office-holders promise delivery after another year of bargaining.
Commercial and political groups pay an ally fee for branded participation; a
small blockade and thrown liquid show that celebration still needs security.?]
[? if year = 2026: Warsaw holds its **25th Equality Parade** on 13 June. Lewica
arrives claiming movement on legal recognition; [? if marriage_reform_settled
or partnership_revision_enacted or partnership_presidential_outcome =
"Signed": this timeline gives the march an enacted reform to judge.?][? if not
marriage_reform_settled and not partnership_revision_enacted and
partnership_presidential_outcome != "Signed": in this timeline the crowd still
has promises rather than a settled national right.?] After twenty-five
parades, symbols are measured against law and everyday safety.?]

[? if pride_social_mood = "favourable": **The public climate is favourable.**
Equality has a working majority and opposition no longer defines every camera
shot. The danger is complacency: a parade can become a summer brand festival
while trans people, young workers and couples wait for institutions.?]
[? if pride_social_mood = "contested": **The public climate is closely
contested.** Pride can normalise equality and also trigger an organised
counter-campaign. Who leads the route will determine whether undecided people
see neighbours, politicians or corporate advertising.?]
[? if pride_social_mood = "hostile": **The public climate remains hostile.**
High backlash makes a visible march a safety operation as well as a festival.
Corporate platforms offer reach and shelter; they can also leave activists
defending employers whose rainbow logos disappear on 1 July.?]

[? if pride_strategy != "No strategy": Last June we chose **[+ pride_strategy +]**.
Activists and employee networks remember whose voice that enlarged and
whose conditions were edited out.?]

- @pride_community_institution
- @pride_movement
- @pride_party
- @pride_corporate
- @pride_worker_compact

@pride_community_institution
title: Transfer the annual Pride institution to a community congress
subtitle: Four movement-led years can secure the route without party or corporate ownership.
view-if: year = 2026 and pride_movement_years >= 4
on-arrival: pride_strategy = "Community-governed Pride"; pride_movement_years += 1; movement_autonomy += 10; progressive_credibility += 7; feminist_trust += 7; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_support += 8; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_left_ownership += 5; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_backlash -= 3; minority_safety += 6; organiser_energy += 5; public_trust += 3; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_support += 11; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_salience += 9; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_backlash += 5; news_headline = "A community congress secures Pride beyond parties and annual sponsors"

Queer organisations, local marches, accessibility teams and employee groups
elect the congress that owns the date, safety rules and political demands.
Parties and companies may participate, but neither can buy the speaking order
or remove a demand from the common platform.

[? if pride_social_mood = "hostile": The hostile climate still follows the
route, but the institution can fund legal defence and security without waiting
for a politician or brand to discover courage in June.?][? if
pride_social_mood = "contested": A contested public sees a stable civic
institution rather than a recurring argument over patronage.?][? if
pride_social_mood = "favourable": Favourable opinion becomes durable capacity
instead of a reason for sponsors and ministers to declare the work finished.?]

- @return_to_dated_desk

@pride_movement
title: Put queer organisations first and the party logistics behind them
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Build movement autonomy and safety with less control over the televised message.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The party lacks one resource for transport, legal aid and accessibility.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; pride_strategy = "Movement-led Pride"; pride_movement_years += 1; movement_autonomy += 7; organiser_energy += 5; feminist_trust += 6; progressive_credibility += 4; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_support += 8; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_left_ownership += 2; minority_safety += 4; spring_dissent -= 3; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_support += 8; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_salience += 8; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_backlash += 6; news_headline = "Lewica supplies Pride infrastructure without taking its stage"

The party pays for accessible transport, legal observers, water and security.
Movement organisations choose the front row, speakers and demands; Lewica MPs
march where the organisers place them.

[? if pride_social_mood = "hostile": Under hostile conditions, the safety
network matters more than our logo. Activists see a party willing to remain
after the counter-demonstrators arrive.?][? if pride_social_mood = "contested":
In contested terrain, participants rather than spokespeople become the public
face of equality.?][? if pride_social_mood = "favourable": On favourable
terrain, surrendering the stage prevents a popular festival from becoming a
party victory lap.?]

- @return_to_dated_desk

@pride_party
title: Lead a single Lewica platform from the front of Pride
subtitle: Maximise equality ownership and visibility; accept the charge of turning a movement into a party auxiliary.
on-arrival: pride_strategy = "Party-led Pride"; pride_party_years += 1; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_left_ownership += 7; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_salience += 5; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_support += 5; progressive_credibility += 3; media_capacity += 5; spring_strength += 2; spring_dissent -= 2; movement_autonomy -= 5; feminist_trust -= 2; public_trust -= 1 if pride_social_mood = "hostile"; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_support += 9; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_salience += 10; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_backlash += 8; news_headline = "Lewica puts its leaders and legislative promises at the front of Pride"

Lewica's platform gathers the party's ministers, MPs and local organisers
behind one legislative demand. Nobody can say the parliamentary Left hid from
the issue.

[? if pride_social_mood = "hostile": The hostile climate makes the platform a
target and a shield. Supporters welcome the courage; organisers fear that one
party's polling now governs their safety and message.?][? if
pride_social_mood = "contested": The contested audience hears a clear demand
and sees every movement argument compressed into a party broadcast.?][? if
pride_social_mood = "favourable": In favourable conditions, the front row
looks less like courage than an attempt to claim credit for a social change
larger than Lewica.?]

- @return_to_dated_desk

@pride_corporate
title: Welcome every corporate platform that signs the equality declaration
subtitle: Buy reach, jobs-network visibility and safer logistics; leave wages and rainbow-washing outside the gate.
on-arrival: pride_strategy = "Corporate ally parade"; pride_corporate_years += 1; media_capacity += 6; organiser_energy += 4; minority_safety += 3; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_support += 8; public_trust += 2; movement_autonomy -= 5; labor_credibility -= 3; union_trust -= 2; progressive_credibility -= 2; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_backlash += 1; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_support += 8; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_salience += 4; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_backlash += 2; news_headline = "Corporate platforms enlarge Pride and narrow its test of allyship"

Banks, technology firms, retailers and entertainment companies provide
vehicles, staff networks and a reach no party campaign could purchase. Queer
workers see senior colleagues march openly and gain evidence that workplace
norms can change.

The declaration asks about discrimination and nothing about contracts, union
rights or whether the same company funds hostile media after Pride month.
[? if pride_social_mood = "hostile": In a hostile climate that compromise
provides real safety and a rare picture of mainstream support.?][? if
pride_social_mood = "contested": In contested terrain the brands help
normalise Pride while making its demands easier to dismiss as marketing.?][?
if pride_social_mood = "favourable": On favourable terrain the logos add less
courage than advertising; activists ask why the movement still sells the best
places on its route.?]

- @return_to_dated_desk

@pride_worker_compact
title: Require a queer-worker compact from every branded platform
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Trade some corporate reach for union access, transparent giving and year-round workplace duties.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: The party cannot audit employers or replace withdrawn logistics.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; pride_strategy = "Queer-worker compact"; pride_worker_compact_years += 1; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_left_ownership += 5; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_support += 5; movement_autonomy += 3; progressive_credibility += 4; labor_credibility += 4; union_trust += 3; opzz_cooperation += 2; workers_support += 2; media_capacity -= 1; organiser_energy += 2; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_support += 10; public_mood_pending_social_spending_support += 5; public_mood_pending_lgbt_equality_salience += 6; news_headline = "Pride makes corporate allyship answer to queer workers"

Any branded platform must publish its donation, recognise an employee network,
name a route for reporting discrimination and meet the union where one exists.
Firms that will not accept the compact may march without promotion or leave.

Some large platforms withdraw, taking vehicles and publicity with them.
[? if pride_social_mood = "hostile": The hostile climate makes every lost
sponsor painful, but queer workers gain protection that lasts past the route.?]
[? if pride_social_mood = "contested": A contested public sees equality tied
to ordinary workplace dignity rather than an imported corporate fashion.?]
[? if pride_social_mood = "favourable": Favourable opinion gives the movement
room to raise the price of allyship from a June logo to a year-round rule.?]

- @return_to_dated_desk

@return_to_dated_desk
title: File the annual march and return to the dated desk
call: poland_normalize
go-to: poland_legacy_event_desk if poland_legacy_event_phase = 1; poland_polling if year <= 2021 and poland_legacy_event_phase = 0; poland_events_2021_2023.router if year >= 2022 and year <= 2023; poland_event_queue if year >= 2024
